#497 -- PREDICTED kill-table scores, written BEFORE the definitive table finished.
seat: opus5.  Instrument: 35-row detector, md5 d04d6bc42baf0600e65c4417a535cb41.

Written for the same reason PRETABLE.out is written first: a prediction recorded
after the result is not a prediction.  Its own mtime is the order proof, and the
table's logs are timestamped after it.  Where measurement disagrees, THE TABLE
WINS and the disagreement is the interesting part -- it is reported, not quietly
reconciled.

 mutant        pred   predicted RED rows and why
 ------------- -----  -------------------------------------------------------
 FIX           35/35  clean by construction
 a_prefix      15/35  18 per-site (sw surv, sw lat1, lw surv x6) + 2 xsite.
                      The 6 `ld kill' rows stay GREEN: pre-fix, a len=8 read
                      already dies at the read arm, which this round never
                      touches -- so they are green on BOTH sides by design.
 c_null        CONTROL=DEAD.  Score is NOT the discriminator (rows wanting
                      `exit0' pass against /bin/true).  Clause (c) is satisfied
                      by the identity rows failing, not by the fraction.

 b_all         15/35  same 20 rows as a_prefix.  Distinct from it because the
                      enum/field/comments REMAIN -- so it is the defect without
                      the infrastructure change, a purer clause-(b) test than
                      the pre-fix binary.  Verified verbatim: b_all differs from
                      the pre-fix source ONLY in comments, enum and field.
 b_<site> x6   30/35  3 rows for that site (sw surv, sw lat1, lw surv) + BOTH
                      xsite rows, because the reinstated exit(1) kills the
                      shared cross-site session at whichever point it is hit.
                      That site's `accept' and `ld kill' stay GREEN: both use
                      len=8, which passes the width guard either way.

 d_andwrite    29/35  the 6 `lw surv'.  if(X) -> if(X && Y) on the dominating
                      predicate: a len=4 READ stops being declined and falls
                      through to the still-live read kill.   [MEASURED 24/30 on
                      the superseded 30-row instrument, same 6 rows.]
 d_growth      29/35  the 6 `accept'.  Guard-only growth swallowing legal
                      8-byte writes at offsets with bit 3 set.  This is the
                      de-escalation accept-row FAILABILITY proof: if these rows
                      do not red here, they are vacuous.
 d_ifnotelse   29/35  the 6 `lw surv'.  Decline kept, its REPAIR nulled: a
                      declined access falls through into the arms again, so a
                      len=4 read reaches the read kill.  NOTE the len=4 WRITE
                      still survives (it just performs a short store), so
                      `sw surv' does NOT catch this -- only the read side does.
 d_nolatch     29/35  the 6 `sw lat1' (count 2, not 1).  `xsite 6msg' stays
                      GREEN -- it touches each site once, so an unlatched build
                      still emits exactly six.  Complementary rows, by design.
 d_noset       29/35  the 6 `sw lat1', same reason.
 d_sharedbit   33/35  BOTH xsite rows only.  Every per-site row runs in its own
                      session where the shared bit is still clear, so all 24
                      stay green.  This is precisely why a cross-site row is
                      needed at all.
 d_bitcollide  33/35  BOTH xsite rows (`6msg' = 5).  The subtle form: one
                      duplicated enumerator.  Same blindness as above.
 e_wrongsite   33/35  BOTH xsite rows: TLB_B's literal count goes to 0, so
                      6msg = 5 and order sees a -1 position.  `tlb_b sw lat1'
                      in ITS OWN session also counts 0 -> so possibly 32/35.
                      *** I am genuinely unsure between 32 and 33 here and am
                      recording the uncertainty rather than picking. ***

 d_returnzero  35/35  *** PREDICTED TO ESCAPE THE WHOLE TABLE. ***  It declines
                      by `return 0', which memory_rw coerces to -1 and turns
                      into a guest EXCEPTION_DBE.  The host process still
                      SURVIVES, so `sw surv'/`lw surv' -- which observe only the
                      exit status -- cannot see it.
                      This is NOT a surprise: PRETABLE.out section 2 recorded
                      claim C2's DBE half as an EXPLICIT NO-ROW before the table
                      ran.  If it escapes, that is a PREDICTED, DISCLOSED hole,
                      and it points at a real weakness in my own row set:
                      claim C1 says the tail "answers a READ with odata (0)" and
                      my `lw surv' row observes survival ONLY, not the 0.
                      IF IT ESCAPES I INTEND TO CLOSE IT IN-ROUND UNDER L1
                      (pre-ship construction) by strengthening the read rows to
                      check the returned VALUE, not merely the exit status.


