--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE ONE ESCAPE, AND WHY IT IS THE ROUND'S MOST USEFUL MEASUREMENT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
`d_returnzero' scored 35/35: it escaped EVERY row.  It keeps the width guard and
keeps the decline, and changes only what happens next -- `return 0' instead of
falling through to the function's common tail.  memory_rw coerces that to -1 and
raises EXCEPTION_DBE in the GUEST, so the HOST process still exits 0 and every
survival-based row stays green.

That is a clause-(d) NULL-REPAIR: the guard is kept and its repair -- reaching
the tail so a declined read returns a defined 0 -- is nulled.  By the floor that
is L2, repaired IN-ROUND however late it is found.

IT WAS DISCLOSED BEFORE IT WAS MEASURED.  PRETABLE.out section 2 recorded claim
C2's DBE half as an EXPLICIT NO-ROW, with its reason, before table 1 ran; this
file predicted the full escape before it happened.  So the sequence is
disclose -> predict -> measure -> repair, not discover-and-quietly-patch.

THE REPAIR, measured in table 2: the read rows preset the destination register to
0xdeadbeef instead of 0, so "the tail ran and wrote odata (0)" and "the load never
completed" stop being the same observation.  Predicted d_returnzero 29/35 there.

*** THE GENERAL LESSON, which is the carrier's own #489 finding reproduced from a
new direction: A SURVIVAL ORACLE AT A REFUSAL-EFFECT SITE IS VACUOUS BY
CONSTRUCTION.  Both the correct fix and this mutant leave the host alive, so
`process exited 0' cannot discriminate them.  The CHANNEL check is supposed to
catch exactly this at WRITING time by asking what observable differs under the
named mutant -- and it half-worked: it made me flag the DBE claim as a no-row,
but it did not make me notice that the row for the OTHER claim (C1, "the tail
answers a READ with odata (0)") was observing survival rather than the 0 that
claim actually asserts.  A row must observe the CLAIM, not the aftermath. ***

