--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE REPAIR, MEASURED END TO END -- the point of running two tables
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   d_returnzero on v1 (survival-only rows) : 35/35  FULL ESCAPE
   d_returnzero on v2 (value-checking rows): 29/35  KILLED, the six `lw surv'

The v2 evidence line is the cleanest artifact this round produced:

   tlb_a lw surv    got exit0/0xdeadbeef    want exit0/0x0

*** THE EXIT STATUS IS IDENTICAL ON BOTH SIDES. *** Correct code and mutant both
leave the host alive; only the untouched sentinel shows the guest load never
completed.  That is what "a survival oracle at a refusal-effect site is vacuous
by construction" looks like when you actually measure it, and it is why the
strengthening was a floor repair (L2) rather than polish.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MY TWO PREDICTION MISSES, and they share one signature
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 d_accept4  predicted 23/35 (12 rows red), measured 21/35 (14 rows red).
            The 12 I named -- 6 `sw lat1', 6 `lw surv' -- all red exactly as
            reasoned.  I FORGOT the two cross-site rows: with the decline never
            firing, NO message is emitted anywhere, so `xsite 6msg' reads 0 and
            `xsite order' finds nothing to order.
            This was my SECOND arithmetic slip on this one mutant; I had already
            corrected 29 -> 23 before the run, and the answer was 21.

 e_swapsite predicted 34/35 (only `xsite order'), measured 32/35.
            `xsite order' RED and `xsite 6msg' GREEN -- SO THE MUTANT ACHIEVED
            ITS PURPOSE: it isolates the anchor row, proving `xsite order' is not
            vacuous.  But `tlb_b sw lat1' and `tlb_c sw lat1' also red, because
            each per-site row counts its OWN literal and the swap makes TLB_B's
            single-site session emit TLB_C's text.

 THE COMMON SIGNATURE: in both cases I reasoned correctly about the row I was
 TARGETING and forgot the other rows that read the SAME transcript.  The
 mechanism was right both times; the blast radius was under-counted both times.
 A mutant's kill set is not the row it was designed for -- it is every row whose
 channel that edit perturbs, and cross-site rows perturb easily because they
 observe an aggregate.

 Neither miss changes a disposition: both mutants are killed, nothing escaped
 that was predicted to die, and no row was found vacuous.

